When a new consumer joins a group (or an existing one leaves, or crashes), Kafka triggers a **rebalance** — partitions are reassigned across the group's current members. A simplified view of what happens:

```
// Before: group has 2 consumers, 4 partitions
Consumer A: partitions [0, 1]
Consumer B: partitions [2, 3]

// Consumer C joins the group -> rebalance triggered
// Group coordinator reassigns partitions across all 3 members

// After rebalance:
Consumer A: partitions [0]
Consumer B: partitions [2, 3]
Consumer C: partitions [1]
```

During a rebalance, consumers typically pause processing until the new assignment is settled — a brief availability gap that's proportional to how many partitions and consumers are involved. Frequent rebalances (consumers joining/leaving often, or one consumer repeatedly timing out and rejoining) can visibly hurt throughput, which is why unnecessary consumer churn — not tuning session timeouts correctly, or deploying consumer instances too aggressively — is a common source of Kafka latency complaints that have nothing to do with the brokers themselves.
